{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "number": 41,
  "title": "Prop 41 \u2014 Tax audits and spending limits: more than an oversight measure",
  "fps": 30,
  "frames": 6079,
  "checked": "2026-10-19",
  "narration": "asset://b9f08cdb59b901d663c8588b0533f6996556a4022a1b5f8c7234378088b7188f.wav",
  "prompter_words": "words-global.json",
  "script": [
    {
      "name": "The big picture",
      "narration": "Proposition forty-one combines audits with changes to state spending-limit rules. A special tax dedicates its money to a particular purpose, unlike revenue that can be used generally. The measure concerns new or increased state special taxes. California already has an independent state auditor and a constitutional spending limit. This proposal adds reviews at particular stages and could restrict the ability to exempt new special-tax spending from that limit."
    },
    {
      "name": "An illustrative example",
      "narration": "Imagine a program manager proposing a dedicated funding measure. Suppose a citizen initiative proposes a new tax for a public program. After supporters collect a quarter of the required signatures, the auditor would review the program, even though the initiative might never qualify. An auditor examines how the program uses its money. If the initiative reaches the ballot, voters receive an audit summary in the voter guide. That could provide useful information, but producing the review still takes staff and money."
    },
    {
      "name": "How it would work",
      "narration": "The initial audit must identify ways to reduce annual program costs by ten percent. Identifying options is not the same as delivering savings or proving that services can stay unchanged. Programs funded by new or increased special taxes approved after January first, twenty twenty-six, would also face recurring audits every four years. Approved taxes generally fund the audits; unsuccessful initiatives can leave costs to the General Fund. The analyst expects audit costs in the low millions annually, growing over time, plus voter-guide expenses. Savings depend on decisions to implement effective recommendations, so the overall fiscal result is unknown. The spending-limit provisions are a separate issue. Prop forty-one could block Prop forty if both pass and forty-one receives more yes votes, subject to how courts interpret their conflict."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote yes",
      "narration": "A voter might want evidence of efficiency before approving new dedicated taxes, and regular follow-up afterward. They may also prefer keeping new revenue within spending constraints. Those are two distinct reasons to consider, rather than assuming audits are the entire measure."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote no",
      "narration": "You might ask whether extra reviews justify their cost. Another voter could favor oversight but object to mandatory reviews of measures that never qualify, or to limits on funding flexibility. They may also oppose the effect on Prop forty. An audit requirement does not automatically mean waste is eliminated."
    },
    {
      "name": "What polling indicates",
      "narration": "In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 51 percent said yes, 44 percent no, and 5 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not votes already cast. A later September Berkeley survey found 37 percent yes, 33 percent no, and 30 percent undecided. Question wording and methods differ, so comparing polls does not by itself establish a trend."
    },
    {
      "name": "Make your own decision",
      "narration": "Yes adds audits and spending-limit provisions; no keeps the current framework. Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links, and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice."
    }
  ],
